WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.209 --> 00:00:05.730
You're looking at a map of places
that needed indoor cooling in 2019.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:08.579
The redder an area is, the more often
temperatures were hot enough

00:00:08.579 --> 00:00:12.170
that people needed some kind of
cooling to be comfortable indoors.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:15.666
The study also charted this into
the future. When you zoom in,

00:00:15.666 --> 00:00:18.960
you can see how many places that
haven’t needed to cool their buildings,

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.966
will need to. Like here,
in Western Europe—

00:00:21.966 --> 00:00:24.963
by 2040, and by 2070—

00:00:24.963 --> 00:00:27.000
Northern China, and Japan,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.594
and in the Pacific
Northwest of the US.

00:00:29.594 --> 00:00:32.399
Where that change
is already underway:

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:35.379
"Seattle is the least air conditioned
metro area in the US."

00:00:35.379 --> 00:00:38.040
"Most people in this area
do not have air conditioning."

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.870
"We tried to get one,
buy one, and they're all--"

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.320
"Sold out of AC units."

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.899
"Dozens of customers lining up at this
hardware store, all leave empty handed."

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:49.829
The world is on the brink of a massive
boom in demand for air conditioning.

00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:53.131
The number of installed AC units
is expected to skyrocket,

00:00:53.131 --> 00:00:56.838
from 2 billion today,
to almost 6 billion by 2050.

00:00:56.838 --> 00:00:59.502
More air conditioning means more 
consumption of electricity,

00:00:59.502 --> 00:01:01.669
and more emissions that 
warm the planet.

00:01:01.669 --> 00:01:04.067
But all of this comes
with a weird twist.

00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:05.954
This increased need to
cool our homes

00:01:05.954 --> 00:01:10.259
might be an unexpected opportunity
to fix an even bigger problem:

00:01:10.259 --> 00:01:11.722
Fixing the way we heat them.

00:01:11.722 --> 00:01:15.368
With a revolutionary device
called a heat pump.

00:01:16.768 --> 00:01:19.553
The basic mechanics of air
conditioning haven’t changed much

00:01:19.553 --> 00:01:21.254
since it was invented
a century ago.

00:01:21.254 --> 00:01:24.810
A fluid called a refrigerant absorbs
heat from inside the home.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:27.251
That heat is moved and released
outside the home,

00:01:27.251 --> 00:01:29.793
and a fan blows
out the newly cold air.

00:01:29.793 --> 00:01:32.024
And it all runs on electricity.

00:01:32.024 --> 00:01:36.672
That’s also what heat pumps do, too -- with
more or less the exact same technology.

00:01:36.672 --> 00:01:38.603
But they can also do it in reverse:

00:01:38.603 --> 00:01:42.093
Taking heat from the air outside,
and moving it inside.

00:01:42.093 --> 00:01:46.320
MICHAEL THOMAS: Heat pumps suffer
from one of the worst names of all time.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.590
A heat pump not only heats
a home, but it also cools one.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:53.859
That’s Michael Thomas, he runs the energy
research group Carbon Switch.

00:01:53.859 --> 00:01:59.509
THOMAS: I can actually show you
my ductless heat pump right here.

00:01:59.509 --> 00:02:05.250
That is a heat pump working its magic
on this 95 degree day here in Colorado.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.876
In addition to "air source" heat pumps,
that transfer heat from the outside air,

00:02:08.876 --> 00:02:12.033
there are "ground source" heat pumps,
that transfer heat between a home

00:02:12.033 --> 00:02:14.384
and the earth several
feet underground.

00:02:14.384 --> 00:02:17.687
And "water source" heat pumps,
which transfer heat between homes

00:02:17.687 --> 00:02:20.440
and nearby bodies of water.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.217
Heat pumps aren't
brand new technology.

00:02:22.217 --> 00:02:25.410
You may already have one, depending
on where in the world you live.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:29.739
But the real opportunity with heat pumps
isn’t changing how we cool our homes.

00:02:29.739 --> 00:02:33.701
THOMAS: You can sort of imagine
a traditional furnace, or a boiler,

00:02:33.701 --> 00:02:37.310
like a Zippo under a pot
of water in your basement,

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:42.223
really inefficiently burning gas,
or fuel oil, or propane,

00:02:42.223 --> 00:02:46.500
and warming that water up, and sending
it throughout your house and pipes,

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:51.980
or going over a fan that's then
blowing through ducts in your house.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.731
In the US, almost two thirds
of homes are heated this way:

00:02:54.731 --> 00:02:57.270
Burning fossil fuels right
inside our homes.

00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:00.473
Residential, public, and commercial
buildings make up almost a third

00:03:00.473 --> 00:03:02.340
of global CO2 emissions.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:07.391
And heating specifically is responsible for
45% of those building emissions worldwide.

00:03:07.391 --> 00:03:10.394
The impact of cooling
is relatively small.

00:03:10.394 --> 00:03:13.114
But, of course, that’s growing.

00:03:13.114 --> 00:03:14.449
And that’s the opportunity:

00:03:14.449 --> 00:03:19.544
If, instead of traditional ACs, people buy
electric machines that both heat and cool,

00:03:19.544 --> 00:03:22.270
the need for fossil-fuel
heating will go way down.

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:28.017
THOMAS: Heat pumps are one of the most
important climate solutions that we have.

00:03:28.017 --> 00:03:29.889
This map shows the regions
where buildings need

00:03:29.889 --> 00:03:33.442
to be cooled during some parts
of the year and heated during others.

00:03:33.442 --> 00:03:35.260
This is the market for heat pumps.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:38.320
It covers a third of
the world population.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.321
What gets energy experts particularly
excited about heat pumps,

00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:44.108
is how they reduce what they
call "locked-in emissions":

00:03:44.108 --> 00:03:48.160
All of the future carbon emissions that
are caused by decisions made today.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.980
THOMAS: The natural gas furnace that my
parents installed when I was growing up

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.571
is still kicking today.

00:03:54.571 --> 00:04:01.310
So you’re making a decision that’s going
to impact climate for 20 or 30 years.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:02.870
One of the obstacles here is that

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:06.083
fossil-fuel heating is often
subsidized by the government,

00:04:06.083 --> 00:04:08.792
while heat pumps can
cost thousands of dollars.

00:04:08.792 --> 00:04:12.009
So heat pumps represent a
particular kind of climate challenge:

00:04:12.009 --> 00:04:14.577
they rely on millions
of individual homeowners,

00:04:14.577 --> 00:04:17.406
each making a specific,
expensive choice.

00:04:17.406 --> 00:04:19.055
But that can change.

00:04:19.055 --> 00:04:22.430
THOMAS: The real solution to this
problem is going to be policy change.

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:27.090
We need to massively increase the
production of heat pumps in this country,

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.333
which is going to be
industrial policy;

00:04:29.333 --> 00:04:34.479
rebates and incentives, which is
going to be budget reconciliation policy;

00:04:34.479 --> 00:04:37.621
We need natural gas to be properly
priced for the negative impact

00:04:37.621 --> 00:04:40.129
that it has on society.

00:04:40.129 --> 00:04:42.770
Heat pumps alone won’t
solve the climate crisis.

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:45.565
While they can extract warmth from
the air when it’s freezing outside,

00:04:45.565 --> 00:04:48.572
many models work less efficiently
in subzero temperatures,

00:04:48.572 --> 00:04:51.855
where traditional furnaces might
still be needed as backup.

00:04:51.855 --> 00:04:53.965
And until our electric grids
are decarbonized,

00:04:53.965 --> 00:04:57.895
heat pumps will still run on
electricity generated by fossil fuels.

00:04:57.895 --> 00:05:01.324
Overall though, heat pumps present
us with a rare opportunity:

00:05:01.324 --> 00:05:05.338
to change something harmful and
long-lasting about the way we live.

00:05:05.338 --> 00:05:07.846
And they show that the big, collective
changes we'll have to make

00:05:07.846 --> 00:05:12.171
to fight climate change, won't all
be massive infrastructure projects.

00:05:12.171 --> 00:05:14.479
Some of them will be
right in our homes.

00:05:14.479 --> 00:05:18.623
THOMAS: We have it in our minds that
the solution to the climate crisis is,

00:05:18.623 --> 00:05:23.090
build a lot of solar farms, build a lot
of wind farms, drive electric vehicles.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:27.067
But there's actually a lot of sort
of boring solutions, like heat pumps,

00:05:27.067 --> 00:05:31.075
that don't get as much press, and
aren't as popular in the mainstream,

00:05:31.075 --> 00:05:35.808
but can deliver really
massive carbon reductions.

